const e={note:"Audit pass. SCRIPT: every native-script string in all nine themes was decomposed character by character with python3 unicodedata.name(). All 73 strings resolve entirely within the Arabic block (U+0600–U+06FF); there are no Latin lookalikes, no combining marks, no zero-width joiners, no stray whitespace. Persian orthography is consistent: FARSI YEH U+06CC throughout and never Arabic yeh U+064A; KEHEH U+06A9 and never Arabic kaf U+0643; PEH U+067E in پیر, TCHEH U+0686 in زایچه, GAF U+06AF in خوابگزاری. The single TEH MARBUTA U+0629 is in حکمة الاشراق, an Arabic book-title that carries it. Two Middle Persian terms in the Cosmology theme — gumēzišn and Činwad puhl — remain in scholarly Latin transliteration in the native field, and their glosses say so and say why. ARITHMETIC: both computed claims verified. خاک مصلی totals 791 (خ 600, ا 1, ک 20, م 40, ص 90, ل 30, ی 10); the letters of بدوح carry 2, 4, 6, 8; the Persian alphabet counts 32 letters against the Arabic 28. SOURCES: four attributions failed checking and were corrected — the Jawāhir-nāma-yi Niẓāmī is by Muḥammad b. Abi'l-Barakāt Jawharī Nīshābūrī, not Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr; the Persian ʿAjāyib al-makhlūqāt is by Muḥammad b. Maḥmūd b. Aḥmad Ṭūsī, edited by Manūchihr Sutūda; Ibn Turka's lettrist summa is the Kitāb al-Mafāḥiṣ, not the Tamhīd al-qavāʿid, which is Ibn ʿArabian metaphysics; and Faḍlallāh Astarābādī came from a family of judges, not from a dyer's household, so the unverified detail is gone. The Ghāyat al-ḥakīm's dates were wrong and are corrected to the text's own claim of 348/959, with the dispute between Fierro's tenth-century dating and the eleventh-century dating of Ritter, Plessner and Pingree now stated rather than suppressed. OVERSTATEMENT: 'that attribution is now generally accepted' is downgraded to widely but not universally accepted; the Niʿmatullāhiyya is no longer called flatly the largest order in Iran; Hujwīrī's book is now the earliest surviving rather than the first; Kraus's dating of the Jabirian corpus is given with Nomanul Haq's dissent; the hadith fraction of one part in forty-six is marked as the best-known of several transmitted figures; the Jalālī calendar's relation to the modern Iranian civil calendar is qualified. RESPECT: a sentence was added to the poetry theme saying plainly that the Magian tavern-keeper is a literary type and not a description of the Zoroastrian community, which has living members who read such essays; the Shams al-maʿārif's 'fearful reputation' is toned down; Zoroastrian, Sufi, Twelver, Shaykhi and Parsi communities are written of in the present tense. NO INSTRUCTION: no operative content anywhere. The talismanic theme names its apparatus and its logic and continues to withhold the invocations and suffumigations of the Ghāya; the Jabirian material on takwīn is named and dropped; istikhāra and shrine incubation are described as an observer describes what he has been shown.",themes:[{slug:"sufism-in-persian",title:"Sufism in Persian",native:"تصوف",translit:"taṣavvuf; and ʿirfān, عرفان — 'gnosis'",summary:"How the mystical literature of Islam changed language in Khurasan, and what the change made possible that Arabic prose had not.",paragraphs:["For three centuries the literature of Sufism was written in Arabic, and a great deal of it was written in Arabic by Persians. Abū Naṣr al-Sarrāj of Tus (d. 378/988) compiled the Kitāb al-Lumaʿ; Kalābādhī of Bukhara (d. c. 380/990, the date variously transmitted) the Kitāb al-Taʿarruf; Qushayrī of Nishapur (d. 465/1072) the Risāla that became the standard handbook of the discipline. The shift when it comes is not a translation but a change of instrument. Hujwīrī of Ghazna, who died at Lahore around 465–70/1073–77, wrote the Kashf al-maḥjūb, the earliest surviving full treatise on Sufism in Persian, and it reads differently from its Arabic predecessors — more argumentative, more personal, more willing to tell a story against itself. Khwāja ʿAbdullāh Anṣārī of Herat (396–481/1006–1089) dictated his Ṭabaqāt al-ṣūfiyya in the Herati dialect, and the Munājāt transmitted under his name are intimate addresses to God in rhymed and cadenced Persian prose that is nearer to verse than to exposition. Aḥmad Ghazālī (d. 520/1126) wrote the Savāniḥ, a sequence of short Persian chapters on love with almost no scaffolding of doctrine at all; his pupil ʿAyn al-Quḍāt Hamadānī, executed at thirty-three in 525/1131, wrote the Tamhīdāt in a Persian of extraordinary pressure. What Persian offered was a language with no independent weight in law or theology, which had for two centuries been the language of poetry. Sufism entering Persian entered verse, and the consequences occupy the rest of this hall.","Two poets fix the form. Sanāʾī of Ghazna, whose death is placed at 525/1131 by most modern scholarship though later dates are transmitted, wrote the Ḥadīqat al-ḥaqīqa wa sharīʿat al-ṭarīqa, 'The Walled Garden of Truth and the Law of the Path', dedicated to Bahrāmshāh — the first sustained didactic masnavi in which mystical doctrine is carried by anecdote, digression and sudden aphorism rather than by chapters. Farīd al-Dīn ʿAṭṭār of Nishapur (c. 540–618/1145–1221) took the method and made a plot of it. In the Manṭiq al-ṭayr, 'The Speech of the Birds', all the birds of the world assemble under the hoopoe to seek their king, the Sīmurgh, and must cross seven valleys to reach him: ṭalab, seeking; ʿishq, love; maʿrifat, gnosis; istighnāʾ, sufficiency, the state in which nothing is needed; tawḥīd, unity; ḥayrat, bewilderment; and faqr u fanāʾ, poverty and annihilation. Most turn back with excellent reasons. Thirty birds arrive, and the Persian for thirty birds is sī murgh — the pun is not decoration but the argument, since what they find at the end of the journey is themselves seen in the light of what they sought. ʿAṭṭār also compiled the Tadhkirat al-awliyāʾ, a prose memorial of the earlier saints that is the source for a great many of the sayings the later tradition treats as canonical.","Jalāl al-Dīn Muḥammad, born at Balkh or nearby in 604/1207 and settled at Konya, was a respectable jurist and preacher until Shams-i Tabrīzī arrived in 642/1244. Shams disappeared for good in 645–46/1247–48, and out of the loss came the Dīvān-i Shams-i Tabrīzī, some thirty-five thousand lines of ghazal and quatrain signed not with the poet's own name but with the vanished friend's — an act with few parallels in any literature. The Masnavī-yi Maʿnavī, six books and roughly twenty-five thousand lines, was dictated over the last decade of his life to Ḥusām al-Dīn Chalabī, who prompted, wrote and kept the work moving. It opens with a reed cut from the reed-bed, crying because it has been cut: the whole doctrine of separation and return is laid out in eighteen lines before any argument begins, and the poem then spends six books declining to summarise itself. Reynold A. Nicholson's edition, translation and commentary (eight volumes, 1925–40) remains the scholarly foundation in English. Samāʿ, audition — listening to poetry and music as a devotional act, with movement — was contested by jurists throughout, defended under conditions by Abū Ḥāmid al-Ghazālī in the Iḥyāʾ, and became the signature of the order that formed around Rumi's memory. The Mevlevi turn was elaborated after him, chiefly under his son Sulṭān Valad, and it is worth saying plainly that when it is performed today, at Konya and elsewhere, its performers regard it as prayer, whatever the audience has been sold.","Ḥāfiẓ of Shiraz (d. 791 or 792/1389–90) is treated in the ninth of these essays, because what he does to the vocabulary belongs there. What belongs here is the institutional shape that grew up around all of this. A ṭarīqa is a silsila, a chain of transmission traced master to master; a novice is a murīd, one who wills, bound to a pīr or murshid by an oath and often invested with the khirqa, the patched cloak; the house is the khānaqāh. The Naqshbandiyya, named for Bahāʾ al-Dīn Naqshband of Bukhara (d. 791/1389), is distinguished by silent rather than vocal dhikr and by the principle of khalvat dar anjuman, solitude within the crowd — an order deliberately built for men who stayed in the world. The Kubrawiyya descends from Najm al-Dīn Kubrā, killed at Khwarazm in 618/1221 during the Mongol assault, whose Fawāʾiḥ al-jamāl records the coloured photisms by which a traveller might read his own state; through his disciple Majd al-Dīn Baghdādī the line produced Najm al-Dīn Rāzī Dāya and the Mirṣād al-ʿibād, still the most useful Persian handbook of the path. The Niʿmatullāhiyya, founded by Shāh Niʿmatullāh Valī (d. 834/1431), whose shrine is at Mahan near Kerman, is generally reckoned the most widely followed Sufi order in Iran today, though it is itself divided into several branches that do not all recognise one another. These are not extinct institutions with archives; they have living shaykhs and living disciples, and this hall describes them accordingly."],terms:[{native:"تصوف",translit:"taṣavvuf",gloss:"Sufism; the abstract noun from ṣūf, wool, after the woollen garment of the early ascetics. In Persian usage it names the institution and its literature, where ʿirfān tends to name the knowledge itself."},{native:"عرفان",translit:"ʿirfān",gloss:"Gnosis, experiential knowing as against ʿilm, acquired knowledge. The preferred term in the later Iranian philosophical tradition, where it names a discipline taught alongside ḥikmat rather than a movement."},{native:"پیر",translit:"pīr",gloss:"'Elder' — the Persian word for the spiritual master, where Arabic has shaykh and murshid. It carries no clerical office; authority rests on the silsila and on the recognition of one's own master."},{native:"مرید",translit:"murīd",gloss:"'One who wills, who desires' — the disciple, defined by his wanting rather than by his learning. The relation to the pīr is contractual, entered by an oath, and the manuals treat its abuses at length."},{native:"سماع",translit:"samāʿ",gloss:"'Listening' — the audition of poetry and music as devotional practice, sometimes with movement. Defended under conditions by al-Ghazālī and attacked by jurists in every century; still performed."},{native:"فنا",translit:"fanāʾ",gloss:"Annihilation, the passing away of the self-regarding self; its complement is baqāʾ, بقا, subsistence in God. ʿAṭṭār makes the pair the last of his seven valleys."},{native:"خانقاه",translit:"khānaqāh",gloss:"The Sufi lodge — residence, refectory, teaching hall and often tomb. An endowed institution with property and a superior, and one of the major forms of medieval Persian architecture."},{native:"نی",translit:"nay",gloss:"The reed flute. Rumi's Masnavī opens by asking the reed why it cries, and answers that it was cut from the reed-bed; the instrument of samāʿ is thus also the poem's first image of the severed soul."}],texts:[{title:"Kashf al-maḥjūb, 'The Unveiling of the Veiled'",note:"ʿAlī b. ʿUthmān Hujwīrī, d. at Lahore c. 465–70/1073–77. The earliest surviving full treatise on Sufism in Persian: doctrine, biography, and an argumentative survey of the schools. English translation by R. A. Nicholson (1911)."},{title:"Ḥadīqat al-ḥaqīqa wa sharīʿat al-ṭarīqa",note:"Sanāʾī of Ghazna, d. 525/1131 on the dating most modern scholarship accepts; dedicated to Bahrāmshāh. The first great didactic mystical masnavi in Persian, and the acknowledged model for ʿAṭṭār and Rumi."},{title:"Manṭiq al-ṭayr, 'The Speech of the Birds'",note:"Farīd al-Dīn ʿAṭṭār of Nishapur; composition traditionally placed around 583/1187, though the date is inferred rather than documented. Some 4,500 couplets. Critical edition by Muḥammad Riżā Shafīʿī Kadkanī (Tehran, 1383/2004)."},{title:"Masnavī-yi Maʿnavī",note:"Jalāl al-Dīn Rumi, 604–672/1207–1273; six books, c. 25,000 lines, dictated to Ḥusām al-Dīn Chalabī. Edited, translated and annotated by R. A. Nicholson in eight volumes (1925–40)."},{title:"Dīvān-i Shams-i Tabrīzī",note:"Rumi's collected ghazals and quatrains, some 35,000 lines, signed with the name of the lost companion rather than the poet's own. Edition by Badīʿ al-Zamān Furūzānfar (Tehran, 1336–46/1957–67)."},{title:"Mirṣād al-ʿibād min al-mabdaʾ ilā'l-maʿād",note:"Najm al-Dīn Rāzī Dāya, completed 620/1223 — the Kubrawi handbook of the path, and the most systematic Persian account of the stages. Translated by Hamid Algar as The Path of God's Bondsmen (1982)."}]},{slug:"illuminationist-philosophy",title:"Illuminationist Philosophy",native:"حکمة الاشراق",translit:"Ḥikmat al-ishrāq — 'the wisdom of illumination'",summary:"Suhrawardī's replacement of substance and accident by degrees of light, his deliberate recovery of an Iranian angelology, and the imaginal world that followed from it.",paragraphs:["Shihāb al-Dīn Yaḥyā Suhrawardī was born at Suhraward near Zanjan about 549/1154, studied at Maragha and Isfahan, and travelled westward until he found a patron in al-Malik al-Ẓāhir, Saladin's son, at Aleppo. He completed the Ḥikmat al-ishrāq in 582/1186, in his early thirties. Five years later, in 587/1191, he was put to death in that city; the order was al-Ẓāhir's, given under pressure from the jurists of Aleppo and, the sources say, from Saladin himself. Those who approved call him al-Maqtūl, the one who was killed; those who did not call him al-Shahīd, the martyr, and the choice of epithet has functioned ever since as a declaration of position. He had first written three works of straightforward Peripatetic philosophy — the Talwīḥāt, the Muqāwamāt, the Mashāriʿ wa'l-muṭāraḥāt — and he says the Ishrāq presupposes them. This matters: illuminationism is not an alternative to logic offered by someone who could not do logic. It is a claim, made by a competent Avicennan, that the Avicennan apparatus fails at a particular point and that a different first principle is required.","The point of failure is definition. A Peripatetic definition proceeds by genus and differentia and must terminate somewhere in a term that cannot be defined; Suhrawardī argues that the terminus should be what is most evident rather than what is most abstract, and that nothing is more evident than light. Light is therefore not a property that things have but what there is. Being is not divided into substance and accident but graded by intensity, so that the whole of reality is one continuum of luminosity running down from the Nūr al-anwār, the Light of Lights, through the anwār qāhira, the dominating lights that are the archangelic order, to the anwār mudabbira, the managing lights that govern bodies — of which the human soul is one, and which he names al-nūr al-isfahbad. Isfahbad is Middle Persian spāhbed, a Sasanian military rank; the word is a manifesto in a single term. Darkness is not a competing substance but privation, and body is barzakh, an isthmus or dark barrier that interrupts light without opposing it. Causation is reconceived to match: a higher light illumines a lower and the lower beholds the higher, and mushāhada, witnessing, does the work that efficient causation did before.","The archangelic order is where he does something no other philosopher in the tradition attempted. He names the dominating lights with the names of the Amesha Spentas — Bahman for Vohu Manah, Urdībihisht for Asha Vahishta, Shahrīvar for Khshathra Vairya, Isfandārmuz for Spenta Ārmaiti, Khurdād and Murdād, properly Amurdād, for Haurvatāt and Amərətāt — and calls the archetype of the sun Hūrakhsh, from Avestan Hvarə-xšaēta. He invokes khurra, the Light of Glory that Avestan calls xvarənah, and its royal form kiyān khurra, as a real luminous grace that descends on kings and sages. And he sets a line of Persian sages — Kayūmarth, Farīdūn, Kay Khusraw, whom he calls the khusrawāniyyūn — beside Hermes, Agathodaemon, Pythagoras, Empedocles and Plato as joint bearers of a single ancient wisdom that Islam inherits. It should be said carefully what this is and is not. Suhrawardī was a Muslim philosopher, and Zoroastrianism was in his time, as it is now, a living religion with its own priesthood, liturgy and law. He was not reviving it, and no Zoroastrian authority has ever claimed him. He was claiming an Iranian philosophical ancestry for a metaphysics of light, and the names he borrowed were for him the names of intelligible realities, not objects of cult.","Between the world of the intellects and the world of the senses he interposes a third: the ʿālam al-mithāl, the world of image or likeness, which has extension, shape and colour but no matter. This is where visions, apparitions, the resurrection body and the events of dream are located, and it is precisely not a synonym for the subjective, since it has its own topography and its own inhabitants. Suhrawardī's short Persian symbolic narratives — ʿAql-i surkh, 'The Red Intellect'; Āvāz-i par-i Jibrāʾīl, 'The Chant of Gabriel's Wing'; Ṣafīr-i sīmurgh, 'The Cry of the Sīmurgh' — set their action there, and give the place a Persian name of unimprovable exactness: Nā-kujā-ābād, 'Nowhere-land'. Henry Corbin, who edited the Opera metaphysica et mystica (1945, 1952, with the Persian volume produced by Seyyed Hossein Nasr in 1970), coined mundus imaginalis in a paper of 1964 to render ʿālam al-mithāl without the English word 'imaginary' and its implication of unreality. The school continued: Shams al-Dīn Shahrazūrī and Quṭb al-Dīn Shīrāzī (d. 710/1311) wrote the standard commentaries, Ibn Kammūna (d. 683/1284) commented on the Talwīḥāt, and in Safavid Isfahan the tradition was recast by Mīr Dāmād (d. 1041/1631), who wrote verse under the pen-name Ishrāq, and by his pupil Mullā Ṣadrā (c. 979–1050/1571–1640). Ṣadrā's al-Ḥikma al-mutaʿāliya fī'l-asfār al-arbaʿa turns Suhrawardī's gradation of light into aṣālat al-vujūd — existence is the reality and quiddity the abstraction — with tashkīk, gradation, internal to existence itself; and it adds ḥarakat javharī, motion in the substance, so that a thing's very being is a continuous becoming. His conclusion that the imaginal faculty survives death, and that the afterlife is imaginally real, is an ishrāqī conclusion reached by a philosopher who had rebuilt the whole system to reach it."],terms:[{native:"نور",translit:"nūr",gloss:"Light — for Suhrawardī not a quality of things but the fundamental reality, admitting of degrees of intensity and requiring no definition because nothing is more self-evident."},{native:"اشراق",translit:"ishrāq",gloss:"Illumination, also the rising of the sun and thus the east; the school's name puns on both, since its wisdom is the light that dawns and the wisdom of the Orient."},{native:"نور الانوار",translit:"Nūr al-anwār",gloss:"The Light of Lights: the first principle, from which all lesser lights derive by illumination rather than by emanation of a different substance."},{native:"انوار قاهره",translit:"anwār-i qāhira",gloss:"The 'dominating lights' — the archetypal order, which Suhrawardī names with the Persian names of the Amesha Spentas. Below them stand the anwār mudabbira, the managing lights that govern bodies."},{native:"عالم مثال",translit:"ʿālam-i mithāl",gloss:"The world of image: extension, shape and colour without matter, where visions and the resurrection body subsist. Corbin's mundus imaginalis renders it while refusing the English 'imaginary'."},{native:"برزخ",translit:"barzakh",gloss:"Isthmus, barrier — Suhrawardī's word for body, which is not a rival substance to light but an interruption of it, dark by privation rather than by nature."},{native:"ناکجاآباد",translit:"Nā-kujā-ābād",gloss:"'Nowhere-land', from the Persian symbolic tales: the country the visionary reaches, real and located but at no point on any map. One of the most quoted coinages in Persian philosophy."},{native:"حرکت جوهری",translit:"ḥarakat-i javharī",gloss:"'Substantial motion' — Mullā Ṣadrā's doctrine that change occurs in the substance itself and not only in its accidents, so that a thing's existence is a continuous renewal."}],texts:[{title:"Ḥikmat al-ishrāq, 'The Philosophy of Illumination'",note:"Suhrawardī, completed 582/1186. Edited by Henry Corbin in Opera metaphysica et mystica II (1952); English translation with the Arabic text by John Walbridge and Hossein Ziai (1999)."},{title:"al-Talwīḥāt, 'The Intimations'",note:"The first of Suhrawardī's Peripatetic works, which the Ishrāq presupposes and criticises. Edited by Corbin in Opera metaphysica et mystica I (1945); commented on by Ibn Kammūna (d. 683/1284)."},{title:"The Persian symbolic narratives",note:"ʿAql-i surkh, Āvāz-i par-i Jibrāʾīl, Ṣafīr-i sīmurgh, Lughat-i mūrān and others: short visionary tales set in the imaginal world. Edited by S. H. Nasr in Opera metaphysica et mystica III (1970); translated by W. M. Thackston (1982)."},{title:"Hayākil al-nūr, 'The Temples of Light'",note:"A short summary of the system in seven sections, extant in Arabic and in a Persian version attributed to Suhrawardī himself; widely commented, notably in the Shawākil al-ḥūr of Jalāl al-Dīn Davānī (d. 908/1502)."},{title:"Sharḥ Ḥikmat al-ishrāq",note:"Quṭb al-Dīn Shīrāzī, d. 710/1311 — the commentary through which the Ishrāq was chiefly read in Iran, printed in the Persian lithograph tradition alongside Shahrazūrī's earlier commentary."},{title:"al-Ḥikma al-mutaʿāliya fī'l-asfār al-ʿaqliyya al-arbaʿa",note:"Mullā Ṣadrā, c. 979–1050/1571–1640 — 'The Four Journeys', the summa of the school of Isfahan, in which the primacy and gradation of existence and substantial motion are set out."}]},{slug:"alchemy",title:"Alchemy",native:"کیمیا",translit:"kīmiyā",summary:"A tradition whose operative masters wrote in Arabic and whose most influential Persian book is about the soul, and the question of whether anyone meant both at once.",paragraphs:["The honest opening is that the great alchemists of Iran did not write in Persian. The Jabirian corpus stands at the head of the tradition: some three thousand titles attributed to Jābir ibn Ḥayyān, presented as an eighth-century pupil of the Imam Jaʿfar al-Ṣādiq, and argued by Paul Kraus in his Jābir ibn Ḥayyān (Cairo, 1942–43) to be the work of many hands over a long period, largely of the late ninth and tenth centuries, and coloured throughout by Ismaili cosmology. Kraus's dating is the standard view and has never been overturned, though Syed Nomanul Haq and others have argued for an earlier historical core; the corpus is composite on any account. Its central doctrine is the ʿilm al-mīzān, the science of the balance. Every substance is composed of the four natures — hot, cold, wet, dry — in fixed proportions, and the proportions are held to be recoverable from the letters of the substance's own name by their abjad values, so that to know a thing's name rightly is to know its constitution and therefore how to alter it. The Books of Balances make alchemy a branch of applied lettrism, which is why the seventh of these essays stands where it does. Transmutation on this account is not the coaxing of one metal into another but the correction of a proportion; and the same theory, extended, produces the corpus's notorious material on takwīn, the artificial generation of living things, which is named here and taken no further.","Against that stands Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Zakariyyā al-Rāzī of Rayy, c. 250–313/854–925, physician, and in Latin Rhazes. His Kitāb al-Asrār, the Book of Secrets, written near the end of his life, is remarkable chiefly for what is missing from it. There is no allegory, no numerology, no oath of secrecy, no Hermetic frame. It classifies substances — bodies, spirits, stones, vitriols, boraxes, salts — then apparatus, the furnace and the crucible and the cucurbit and alembic and the aludel, then operations: distillation, sublimation, calcination, solution, coagulation, the softening of hard bodies. The arrangement is that of a laboratory manual and its temper is experimental in a sense that would be recognised later: procedures are given with quantities, and failures are admitted. Julius Ruska's German version of 1937 remains the standard scholarly access, though Ruska published it under the title of the shorter companion treatise Sirr al-asrār, and the two works are still routinely confused; Gail Marlow Taylor has since translated the text into English. Against al-Rāzī's confidence stands Ibn Sīnā, who in the mineralogical section of the Shifāʾ denied that species of metal can be transmuted at all, allowing only that the artisans produce excellent imitations; that passage travelled to Latin Europe appended to Aristotle's Meteorology and became the sceptics' authority for four centuries. The argument was already fully formed before it left the Persian-speaking world.","In Persian itself the neighbouring science of matter flourished more visibly than alchemy proper. The jawāhir-nāma, the book of gems, is a genuinely Persian genre, standing beside the great Arabic model, Bīrūnī's al-Jamāhir fī maʿrifat al-jawāhir. Muḥammad b. Abi'l-Barakāt Jawharī Nīshābūrī wrote the Jawāhir-nāma-yi Niẓāmī in 592/1196; Naṣīr al-Dīn Ṭūsī compiled the Tansūkh-nāma-yi Īlkhānī for Hulagu; and Abū'l-Qāsim Kāshānī's ʿArāyis al-jawāhir wa nafāʾis al-aṭāyib of 700/1301 is also our best single source for the making of lustre ceramic and the use of cobalt, since its author belonged to the family of Kashan tile-makers. These are books about testing, valuing and imitating stones, and they share a vocabulary and a workshop with the alchemists without sharing the promise. Alchemy proper survives in Persian mostly in manuscript compilations, translations and epitomes of Arabic originals rather than in a canon of edited texts, and it should be said plainly that the Persian alchemical corpus is far less well surveyed than the Arabic; general statements about it are provisional in a way that statements about al-Rāzī are not.","The decisive Persian book bearing the word is not an alchemical treatise at all. Abū Ḥāmid al-Ghazālī, at Tus in the last decade before his death in 505/1111, made a Persian recasting of his own Iḥyāʾ ʿulūm al-dīn and called it Kīmiyā-yi saʿādat, 'The Alchemy of Happiness'. The title fixes a transfer that the whole subsequent literature depends on: the base metal is the soul, the elixir is a discipline, the assay is conduct, and the transmutation is real but not metallurgical. After Ghazālī the vocabulary is common property. Rumi and Ḥāfiẓ use kīmiyā and iksīr constantly, and with the workshop's terms intact — copper and gold, the furnace, the projection of a minute quantity on a great mass; the beloved's glance is the elixir that turns the copper of the self, and the tavern is a furnace. The perennial question is whether the operative and the allegorical readings were ever really rivals. The evidence suggests not. They coexisted in the same century and often in the same library; the poets used technical language accurately because their readers knew the technique; and the allegory has force precisely because for somebody, somewhere in the same city, the operation was literal."],terms:[{native:"کیمیا",translit:"kīmiyā",gloss:"Alchemy, and also the elixir itself. In Persian poetry the word rarely means only the craft: to call something kīmiyā is to say it is both transformative and vanishingly rare."},{native:"اکسیر",translit:"iksīr",gloss:"The elixir, from Greek xērion, a dry powder for wounds. The agent of transmutation, projected in small quantity on a large mass; in the poets, whatever converts the self at a touch."},{native:"سیماب",translit:"sīmāb",gloss:"'Silver-water', quicksilver — the Persian word for mercury, one of the two principles of the sulphur-mercury theory of metals, and the standard image for restlessness."},{native:"کبریت",translit:"kibrīt",gloss:"Sulphur, the active and combustible principle paired with mercury; in the mineral theory the two combine underground in varying purity to yield the seven metals."},{native:"تدبیر",translit:"tadbīr",gloss:"'Management, regimen' — the technical word for an alchemical operation or the whole ordered sequence of them, borrowed from medicine, where it means the regulation of a patient."},{native:"میزان",translit:"mīzān",gloss:"Balance, scales. In the Jabirian corpus the ʿilm al-mīzān is the doctrine that a substance's proportion of the four natures can be derived from the letters of its name."},{native:"جوهر",translit:"javhar",gloss:"Substance or essence, and also a gem — the same word serves philosophy and the jewellers' manuals, which is one reason the jawāhir-nāma sits so close to the alchemical literature."}],texts:[{title:"Kitāb al-Asrār, 'The Book of Secrets'",note:"Abū Bakr al-Rāzī of Rayy, d. 313/925. Substances, apparatus and operations set out as a working manual, without allegory. German version by Julius Ruska (1937), published under the title of the shorter companion treatise Sirr al-asrār; English translation by Gail Marlow Taylor."},{title:"Kutub al-mawāzīn, 'The Books of Balances'",note:"Part of the corpus attributed to Jābir ibn Ḥayyān; the doctrine that the proportions of the four natures in a substance are legible in the abjad values of its name. Analysed by Paul Kraus, Jābir ibn Ḥayyān (Cairo, 1942–43)."},{title:"Kitāb al-Shifāʾ, the mineralogical section",note:"Ibn Sīnā, d. 428/1037. The denial that metallic species can be transmuted; known in Latin as De congelatione et conglutinatione lapidum, translated c. 1200 by Alfred of Sareshel and circulated with Aristotle's Meteorology."},{title:"Jawāhir-nāma-yi Niẓāmī",note:"Muḥammad b. Abi'l-Barakāt Jawharī Nīshābūrī, 592/1196 — a Persian book of gems: identification, testing, imitation and value. Edited by Īraj Afshār with Rasūl Daryāgasht (Tehran, 1383/2004)."},{title:"ʿArāyis al-jawāhir wa nafāʾis al-aṭāyib",note:"Abū'l-Qāsim ʿAbdallāh Kāshānī, 700/1301. Persian; on gems and on the making of lustre ceramic and tilework, by a member of the Kashan potters' family."},{title:"Kīmiyā-yi saʿādat, 'The Alchemy of Happiness'",note:"Abū Ḥāmid al-Ghazālī, d. 505/1111 — the author's own Persian recasting of the Iḥyāʾ, and the book that made kīmiyā a term for the transformation of the self in Persian for good."}]},{slug:"astrology",title:"Astrology",native:"احکام نجوم",translit:"aḥkām-i nujūm — 'the judgements of the stars'",summary:"A Sasanian inheritance, a doctrine that made history a function of two slow planets, and the astronomers who reformed the calendar while doubting the art.",paragraphs:["Sasanian Iran was the junction at which Greek and Indian astrology met and were combined, and the fact of that junction is the foundation of everything afterwards. Royal astronomical tables, the Zīg ī Šahriyārān — the Zīj al-Shāh of the Arabic sources — were compiled and revised under Khusraw I in the sixth century; Greek technical literature was translated into Middle Persian, and the loss of those translations is one of the great gaps in the history of the science. Its scale can be measured from a single survival: the Carmen astrologicum of Dorotheus of Sidon, a first-century Greek didactic poem, is extant in full only in the Arabic version made about 800 by ʿUmar ibn al-Farrukhān al-Ṭabarī from a Middle Persian intermediary, and was edited from that Arabic by David Pingree in 1976. Pingree's studies of the transmission remain the indispensable scholarship. The religious cosmology, meanwhile, ran the other way. In the Bundahišn the fixed stars are Ohrmazd's army, arrayed under four commanders of the quarters — Tištar, Sirius, in the east; Haftōring, the Great Bear, in the north; Wanand, Vega, in the west; and Sadwēs in the south, whose identification is disputed — while the seven planets are Ahriman's counter-force, each assigned an opponent among them, since a body that wanders is a body that has broken rank. With Gōzihr, the dragon of the eclipse, and the tailed Mušparīg, they make ten adversaries. That a culture whose sacred books called the planets brigands should also have produced the finest horoscopic astrologers of the age is not a paradox to be dissolved; the compilers of the Bundahišn and the court astrologers were not colleagues, and the tension between them was real and never resolved.","Abū Maʿshar Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Balkhī (171–272/787–886), from Balkh, who died at Wasit and whom Latin Europe knew as Albumasar, gave the tradition its philosophy of history. Saturn and Jupiter conjoin roughly every twenty years; successive conjunctions fall within one triplicity of the zodiac for about two hundred and forty years before shifting to the next; and the cycle returns to its beginning after about nine hundred and sixty. Abū Maʿshar arranged these into small, middle and great conjunctions and made them the armature of political time: the twenty-year meeting marks a change of ruler, the shift of triplicity a change of dynasty, the full return the appearance of a prophet or a religion. The doctrine is set out in the Kitāb al-Milal wa'l-duwal, 'On Religions and Dynasties', which became De magnis coniunctionibus, and it rested on a chronological framework worked out in the lost Kitāb al-Ulūf, reconstructed by Pingree as The Thousands of Abū Maʿshar (1968). His Kitāb al-Madkhal al-kabīr, the Great Introduction of 848, translated by John of Seville in 1133 and again by Hermann of Carinthia in 1140, is the single book most responsible for the shape of Latin astrology; it has been edited and translated by Keiji Yamamoto and Charles Burnett (2019).","At court the practice was continuous and institutional. The horoscope figure, the zāyecheh, was drawn in the medieval Islamic and Persian manner as a square divided by its diagonals into twelve compartments, not as a wheel; the round chart is a later European habit, and the difference is visible at a glance in any Persian manuscript. Baghdad was founded in 145/762 on a moment chosen by Nawbakht the Persian and Māshāʾallāh. Nizāmī ʿArūżī's Chahār Maqāla, written about 550/1155, treats the astrologer as one of the four professions no court can do without — alongside the secretary, the poet and the physician — and its chapter on the munajjim, with its anecdotes of the men who held the post, is the best portrait we have of the job as a job. Hulagu founded the observatory at Maragha in 657/1259 at Naṣīr al-Dīn Ṭūsī's urging, and his interest in it was frankly astrological; the Zīj-i Īlkhānī followed in 1272. Ulugh Beg's observatory at Samarkand produced the Zīj-i Sulṭānī, completed in 1437 and corrected until his death, the most accurate star catalogue made before the telescope. And the doctrine of conjunctions became a title: Tīmūr took the style Ṣāḥib-Qirān, Lord of the Conjunction, and it was claimed afterwards by Shāh Jahān and by Fatḥ-ʿAlī Shāh. A dynasty that could show itself born at the turn of a nine-hundred-and-sixty-year cycle was not merely lucky but scheduled.","Two figures show the two faces of the profession at once. Abū Rayḥān al-Bīrūnī (362–c. 440/973–c. 1048) wrote the finest textbook the science ever had, the Kitāb al-Tafhīm li-awāʾil ṣināʿat al-tanjīm of 420/1029, which he produced in both Arabic and Persian and which sets out arithmetic, geometry, astronomy and the technical apparatus of judgements in five graded books — and he is at the same time the sharpest critic of the astrologers in the language, contemptuous of their pretensions where his own tables were exact. ʿUmar Khayyām of Nishapur (c. 440–526/1048–1131) held the same double office. Summoned by Malikshāh and Niẓām al-Mulk, he worked from about 467/1074 at the observatory at Isfahan on a reform of the solar calendar; the Jalālī calendar promulgated in 1079 uses an intercalation of eight leap days in thirty-three years, giving a mean year closer to the tropical year than the Gregorian, and the Solar Hijri calendar in civil use in Iran today descends from it, though it now fixes the year by the observed vernal equinox rather than by Khayyām's cycle. He solved cubic equations by the intersection of conic sections, and wrote a searching commentary on Euclid's fifth postulate. The same man was expected to cast horoscopes, and the anecdotes preserved by Nizāmī ʿArūżī and others report him doing so without much conviction, holding that the causes of things are hidden. The astronomer and the astrologer were one office. The scepticism was often internal to it, and the calendar — checkable, correctable, indifferent to patronage — is what that scepticism produced when it was given something it could actually measure."],terms:[{native:"احکام نجوم",translit:"aḥkām-i nujūm",gloss:"'The judgements of the stars' — judicial astrology, the interpretative art, as distinguished from ʿilm-i hayʾa, the mathematical astronomy of the spheres. The distinction is technical, not moral, and the same men practised both."},{native:"زایچه",translit:"zāyecheh",gloss:"The horoscope figure or natal chart, drawn as a square divided by diagonals into twelve compartments in the Persian and Arabic manuscripts, rather than as the circular wheel of later European practice."},{native:"قران",translit:"qirān",gloss:"Conjunction — properly of Saturn and Jupiter, the two slowest visible planets, whose meetings every twenty years supply the periodisation of Abū Maʿshar's history."},{native:"صاحبقران",translit:"Ṣāḥib-Qirān",gloss:"'Lord of the Conjunction', the title taken by Tīmūr and claimed after him by Shāh Jahān and Fatḥ-ʿAlī Shāh: a sovereignty asserted not by descent but by the calendar of the heavens."},{native:"طالع",translit:"ṭāliʿ",gloss:"'The rising' — the ascendant, the degree of the ecliptic on the eastern horizon at the moment in question, and by extension a person's fortune. To 'have a good ṭāliʿ' is ordinary Persian for being lucky."},{native:"زیج",translit:"zīj",gloss:"A set of astronomical tables with the canons for using them, from Middle Persian zīg. The zīj is the working instrument of the science and its most exacting product."},{native:"منجم",translit:"munajjim",gloss:"The astronomer-astrologer, one of the four professions Nizāmī ʿArūżī holds a court cannot function without. A salaried post with responsibilities for the calendar as well as for judgements."},{native:"بندهشن",translit:"Bundahišn",gloss:"'Primal creation' — the ninth-century Pahlavi compendium in which the fixed stars are Ohrmazd's troops and the seven planets Ahriman's, each bound to a stellar opponent."}],texts:[{title:"Kitāb al-Madkhal al-kabīr, 'The Great Introduction'",note:"Abū Maʿshar al-Balkhī, 848. The philosophical defence and systematic exposition of astrology; translated into Latin by John of Seville (1133) and Hermann of Carinthia (1140). Edition and English translation by Keiji Yamamoto and Charles Burnett (2019)."},{title:"Kitāb al-Milal wa'l-duwal, 'On Religions and Dynasties'",note:"Abū Maʿshar's doctrine of great conjunctions, and the source of Latin De magnis coniunctionibus. Arabic text with the Latin of John of Seville, edited by Yamamoto and Burnett (2000)."},{title:"Carmen astrologicum",note:"Dorotheus of Sidon, first century, extant in full only in the Arabic of ʿUmar ibn al-Farrukhān al-Ṭabarī made from a Middle Persian version. Edited by David Pingree (Leipzig, 1976) — the clearest single measure of the Sasanian transmission."},{title:"Kitāb al-Tafhīm li-awāʾil ṣināʿat al-tanjīm",note:"Abū Rayḥān al-Bīrūnī, 420/1029, in Persian and Arabic — the most rigorous textbook of the science, by a scholar openly contemptuous of the astrologers' claims. English translation of the Arabic by R. Ramsay Wright (1934)."},{title:"Chahār Maqāla, 'The Four Discourses'",note:"Nizāmī ʿArūżī Samarqandī, c. 550/1155. The secretary, the poet, the astrologer and the physician; the third discourse is the best account of the court astrologer's actual employment. Translated by E. G. Browne."},{title:"Zīj-i Īlkhānī",note:"Naṣīr al-Dīn Ṭūsī and his colleagues at the Maragha observatory, completed 1272 — tables in Persian, produced by an institution founded on Hulagu's astrological interest and outlasting it."}]},{slug:"talismanic-magic",title:"Talismanic Magic",native:"طلسمات",translit:"ṭilismāt (sing. ṭilism, طلسم)",summary:"The science of occult properties, the timing that made them operative, and a literature whose manuscripts outnumber its editions by an order of magnitude.",paragraphs:["A ṭilism — the word is Greek telesma, a consecrated object, probably by way of Syriac — is a made thing charged to produce an effect at a distance. Its defenders were careful to distinguish it from siḥr, sorcery, and the distinction they drew was causal rather than moral: sorcery works by pact or by illusion, whereas the talisman works by natural properties, and is therefore a science. The premise of that claim is ʿilm al-khavāṣṣ, the science of properties. Alongside the four elemental qualities, every substance was held to possess a khāṣṣiyya, a specific virtue not deducible from its composition — the standard example, conceded by everyone including the sceptics, is the lodestone, which attracts iron for no reason the physics of hot and cold can supply. If such properties exist, they can in principle be catalogued, and if catalogued, combined. What converts a compounded property into a talisman is timing: the celestial configuration supplies the form, the metal or stone supplies the matter, and the object is made at the moment when the two correspond. Talismanic craft is therefore parasitic on astrology in the strict sense — remove the horoscope and nothing remains but a piece of engraved metal.","The central book of the tradition is the Ghāyat al-ḥakīm, 'The Aim of the Sage', compiled in al-Andalus; the text itself claims completion in 348/959, having been begun after a companion work finished in 343/954. It was for centuries attributed to the mathematician Maslama al-Majrīṭī, on Ibn Khaldūn's authority, an attribution nobody now defends, since al-Majrīṭī would have been a child. Maribel Fierro argued in 1996 that the author is Maslama b. Qāsim al-Qurṭubī (d. 353/964), who also wrote the companion Rutbat al-ḥakīm and who appears to have introduced the epistles of the Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ into Spain. Her case is widely but not universally accepted: Ritter, Plessner and Pingree all preferred a compilation date in the eleventh century, and the question is not closed. The Arabic was edited by Hellmut Ritter in 1933 and translated into German by Ritter and Martin Plessner in 1962; at the court of Alfonso X, between 1256 and 1258, it was rendered into Castilian and thence into the Latin Picatrix, in which form it did most of its European work. The book travelled east as well as west and was known and discussed across the Persianate world. Its reputation rests on the sections giving planetary invocations and suffumigations, and those are described here and not reproduced; this hall records what the literature contains and what its practitioners understood themselves to be doing, and stops there.","The technical furniture is consistent enough to summarise. Planetary hours divide the interval from sunrise to sunset into twelve unequal parts and the night likewise, the first hour of the day belonging to the planet that names the day and the rest following the Chaldean order of the spheres — a scheme that also, run round seven times, generates the sequence of the days of the week, which is why the calendar itself is a piece of surviving astrological machinery. The metals are assigned: lead to Saturn, tin to Jupiter, iron to Mars, gold to the Sun, copper to Venus, quicksilver to Mercury, silver to the Moon. The wafq, plural awfāq — the magic square, in which every row, column and diagonal yields the same total — is likewise distributed by order, the three-by-three to Saturn, four to Jupiter, five to Mars, six to the Sun, seven to Venus, eight to Mercury, nine to the Moon. The three-by-three is the celebrated buduh, named from the letters b, d, w and ḥ whose abjad values are 2, 4, 6 and 8 and which stand in its four corners; the invented word became a name in its own right and is written on amulets, bowls and doorways across the Persianate world. The objects themselves are various: the khātam or seal-ring; the ḥirz and taʿvīdh worn on the person; inscribed magic-medicinal bowls; and the talismanic shirt, covered in minute script and ruled squares and worn under armour, of which Safavid, Ottoman and Mughal examples survive in the Topkapı and elsewhere and are among the most beautiful things the tradition made.","It must be said how little of this literature has been read. Aḥmad al-Būnī (d. 622/1225, or 630/1232 in some notices) is its most famous name, but the Shams al-maʿārif al-kubrā that circulates under it — the book a Cairo or Tehran bookseller will sell you today, and the one with the forbidding reputation — is a compilation whose manuscripts do not appear before the seventeenth century, stitched together from genuine Bunian material and a great deal else; this was established independently and almost simultaneously by Noah Gardiner and Jean-Charles Coulon, and the authentic corpus turns out to be smaller, more Sufi and considerably less lurid. The same layering is visible in Persian. Ḥusayn Vāʿiẓ Kāshifī of Herat (d. 910/1504–5) wrote the Asrār-i Qāsimī on the five occult sciences whose initials spell the mnemonic kulluhu sirr — kīmiyā, līmiyā, hīmiyā, sīmiyā, rīmiyā — but the text as it circulates carries a large Safavid interpolation, and Maria Subtelny's work has had to separate the two. The Persian situation at large is worse rather than better. The Kitābkhāna-yi Majlis in Tehran, the Āstān-i Quds Rażavī in Mashhad, the Malek library, the Süleymaniye, the British Library and the Berlin collection catalogued by Ahlwardt hold hundreds of Persian talismanic, khavāṣṣ and nīranjāt texts, of which a handful have been edited and rather fewer studied. Serious work has begun — Matthew Melvin-Koushki on Persianate occultism and its place at the Timurid and Safavid centre rather than the margin, Liana Saif on the philosophy of Arabic occult science, Emilie Savage-Smith on the surviving objects — but any general statement about what the Persian talismanic tradition holds is, at present, a statement about a small and unrepresentative sample, and should be offered as such."],terms:[{native:"طلسم",translit:"ṭilism",gloss:"A talisman: a made object charged to act at a distance, from Greek telesma. Its makers distinguished it sharply from siḥr, sorcery, on the ground that it operates by natural properties."},{native:"علم خواص",translit:"ʿilm-i khavāṣṣ",gloss:"The science of specific properties — virtues belonging to a substance but not deducible from its elemental qualities. The lodestone's attraction is the example everyone conceded, and the whole edifice rests on it."},{native:"وفق",translit:"vafq",gloss:"A magic square, in which every row, column and diagonal sums alike; the word means 'agreement, concord'. Also a general term for a numerical arrangement in harmony with a celestial one."},{native:"اوفاق",translit:"awfāq",gloss:"The plural: the set of squares from three-by-three to nine-by-nine, distributed to the seven planets in ascending order from Saturn to the Moon."},{native:"بدوح",translit:"buduh",gloss:"The name formed from the letters b, d, w, ḥ — abjad values 2, 4, 6, 8 — that occupy the corners of the three-by-three square. Written on amulets, bowls and doorways as a word in its own right."},{native:"خاتم",translit:"khātam",gloss:"A seal, a signet-ring, and by extension an engraved talismanic device; khātam-i Sulaymān, the seal of Solomon, is the best known and appears on objects from Spain to Bengal."},{native:"تعویذ",translit:"taʿvīdh",gloss:"An amulet, from a root meaning to take refuge; commonly a written text folded into a case and worn. In ordinary Persian usage the word covers protective writing of any kind."},{native:"حرز",translit:"ḥirz",gloss:"A stronghold, and thence a protective prayer or the amulet carrying it. The Shiʿi tradition knows a number of ḥirz texts transmitted from the Imams and treated as devotional rather than occult."}],texts:[{title:"Ghāyat al-ḥakīm, 'The Aim of the Sage'",note:"Compiled in al-Andalus; the text claims completion in 348/959. Attributed by Maribel Fierro (1996) to Maslama b. Qāsim al-Qurṭubī (d. 353/964), author also of the companion Rutbat al-ḥakīm; Ritter, Plessner and Pingree favoured an eleventh-century date, and the question is open. Arabic edition by Ritter (1933); German translation by Ritter and Plessner (1962)."},{title:"Picatrix",note:"The Latin Ghāya, made from a Castilian version at the court of Alfonso X of Castile between 1256 and 1258, and the channel through which the Arabic material entered European magic. Latin edition by David Pingree (Warburg Institute, 1986)."},{title:"Asrār-i Qāsimī",note:"Ḥusayn Vāʿiẓ Kāshifī of Herat, d. 910/1504–5 — the best-known Persian manual of the five occult sciences, named for the poet Qāsim-i Anvār. The circulating text carries a substantial Safavid expansion; distinguished from Kāshifī's original by Maria Subtelny."},{title:"al-Filāḥa al-Nabaṭiyya, 'Nabatean Agriculture'",note:"Compiled in Arabic by Ibn Waḥshiyya c. 291/904 and presented as a translation from ancient Syriac: agriculture, plant lore and occult properties together, and one of the Ghāya's principal sources."},{title:"Shams al-maʿārif al-kubrā",note:"Circulates under the name of Aḥmad al-Būnī (d. 622/1225) but is a later compilation whose manuscripts do not predate the seventeenth century, as shown by Noah Gardiner and Jean-Charles Coulon; the authentic Bunian corpus is smaller and different in character."},{title:"al-Muqaddima, the chapters on talismans and sorcery",note:"Ibn Khaldūn, 779/1377 — a hostile but exceptionally well-informed survey of what the science claimed and on what grounds, and the source of the long-standing misattribution of the Ghāya to al-Majrīṭī."}]},{slug:"dream-interpretation",title:"Dream Interpretation",native:"تعبیر خواب",translit:"taʿbīr-i khvāb — 'the crossing-over of the dream'",summary:"A recognised science with manuals, a threefold diagnosis before any interpretation, and a mode of knowledge the Sufis were prepared to defend.",paragraphs:["Taʿbīr is built on a root meaning to cross over, and the metaphor is exact: the interpreter carries a meaning across from the image to the thing. It was classed as a science, with a Qur'anic warrant in the story of Yūsuf, who is taught taʾwīl al-aḥādīth, the interpretation of events and reports, and who reads the dreams of the prison and of the king. The hadith corpus supplies both the licence and the caution. The good dream is called a part of prophecy — the best-known report gives the fraction as one part in forty-six, though other figures are transmitted — and dreams are sorted into three kinds before any reading is attempted: the ruʾyā, the true vision, which comes from God; the ḥulm, the disturbing dream, ascribed to Satan; and the dream that is simply ḥadīth al-nafs, the sleeper's own preoccupation replaying itself. This triage is the first operation of the science and it governs everything that follows, including whether a dream should be interpreted at all and to whom it may be told; the manuals warn repeatedly against relating a bad dream, and against interpreting for the wrong person. Interpretation itself is frankly contextual. The same image is read differently for a king and a labourer, in Ramadan and outside it, for the pious and the negligent, and a competent interpreter is one who asks about the dreamer before answering about the dream.","The presiding authority is Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn of Basra (d. 110/728), of the generation after the Companions, remembered as the interpreter without equal. The books printed under his name — Muntakhab al-kalām fī tafsīr al-aḥlām and the various Taʿbīr al-ruʾyā — are later compilations, and the honest position is that his authority is genuine and the books attributed to him are not his. Real early works survive: Ibn Qutayba (d. 276/889) wrote a Taʿbīr al-ruʾyā, and from Nishapur, Abū Saʿd al-Kharkūshī (d. 406/1015 or 407/1016) produced the Kitāb al-Bishāra wa'l-nidhāra, 'Good Tidings and Warning', one of the fullest early manuals — and the same man wrote a handbook of Sufism, the Tahdhīb al-asrār, which shows how naturally the two interests sat together. The late compendium that is still consulted is ʿAbd al-Ghanī al-Nābulusī's Taʿṭīr al-anām fī taʿbīr al-manām (d. 1143/1731), arranged alphabetically by image and continuously in print. In Persian, the anonymous Khvābguzārī, of the sixth or seventh Islamic century, survives in a single manuscript and was edited by Īraj Afshār (Tehran, 1346/1967); it is the earliest substantial Persian dream-book we have, and its very singleness is a fair indication of how much of this literature has not come down.","The philosophers gave the true dream a mechanism, and the mechanism explains why interpretation should be possible at all. On Ibn Sīnā's account, in sleep the outward senses fall quiet and the imaginative faculty is released from their service; a soul sufficiently prepared receives an intelligible influx from the active intellect, which the imagination cannot hold as intelligible and therefore clothes in images drawn from the dreamer's own stock. Interpretation is the reverse operation: the interpreter undresses the image to reach what was received. Suhrawardī and Mullā Ṣadrā located the whole transaction in the ʿālam al-mithāl, which has the effect of making the dream neither subjective nor bodily but placed — an event in a world with its own extension, entered rather than generated. For the Sufis the ruʾyā is straightforwardly a mode of knowledge, subject to its own etiquette and its own tests. Rūzbihān Baqlī of Shiraz (1128–1209) kept what amounts to a visionary diary, the Kashf al-asrār, recording his unveilings with a candour that has few parallels in the tradition; Carl Ernst translated it in 1997 and Firoozeh Papan-Matin with Michael Fishbein edited the Arabic in 2006. Some lineages go further and admit initiation by vision: the Uwaysī transmission, named for Uways al-Qaranī, who is said to have known the Prophet without ever meeting him, in which a living disciple receives from a master long dead. This was always a claim made about particular individuals rather than a normal route of instruction, and the manuals treat it as exceptional.","Istikhāra, 'seeking the best', is the practice by which an ordinary person consults about an ordinary decision. In its hadith-grounded form it is simply a short prayer said before a choice, transmitted from Jābir b. ʿAbdallāh; in Iranian usage it is also commonly performed with the rosary and with the Qur'an opened at random, the outcome read by a person qualified to read it, and jurists have differed over how much weight these latter forms may bear. It is frequently hoped that the answer will arrive in sleep, and where it does not, the seeker may sleep at a shrine in order to be answered there. Incubation of this kind is attested throughout the Persianate world and continues at Mashhad, Qom and many lesser sites; it is a living devotion, and it is described here as an observer describes what he has been shown, not as a technique. Dreams also did public work. Safavid and Qajar sources record dreams that authenticated a shrine, licensed the building of a tomb, confirmed a lineage or ratified a claim to rule, and the dream-report is a recognisable genre of Persian historical writing with its own conventions of setting, witness and confirmation. A historian who dismisses these as fabrications loses the evidence; a historian who takes them as reportage loses the genre. They are best read as what they are — a formal instrument by which a community made a decision reviewable and public."],terms:[{native:"تعبیر",translit:"taʿbīr",gloss:"Interpretation, from a root meaning to cross over: the interpreter carries the meaning from the image to the thing signified. The science and its manuals both take the name."},{native:"خواب",translit:"khvāb",gloss:"Sleep, and the dream in it — the ordinary Persian word, written with an unpronounced vāv after the khe, as in khvāhar and khvāndan. The compound khvāb-guzārī is 'dream-interpreting'."},{native:"رؤیا",translit:"ruʾyā",gloss:"The true dream, the vision — the class of dream held to come from God and admitted to interpretation. The hadith calls it a part of prophecy, the best-known report specifying one part in forty-six."},{native:"حلم",translit:"ḥulm",gloss:"The confused or frightening dream, ascribed in the hadith to Satan; the tradition advises that it be neither related nor interpreted. Distinguishing it from ruʾyā is the interpreter's first task."},{native:"رؤیای صادقه",translit:"ruʾyā-yi ṣādiqa",gloss:"'The veridical dream' — the technical term for a vision that proves true, and the category around which the philosophical defence of dreaming as knowledge was constructed."},{native:"استخاره",translit:"istikhāra",gloss:"'Seeking the best' — the prayer for guidance before a decision, transmitted from Jābir b. ʿAbdallāh; in Iranian practice also performed with the rosary or with the Qur'an, forms over which jurists have differed."},{native:"خوابگزاری",translit:"khvāb-guzārī",gloss:"Dream-interpreting, the Persian term for the practice and the title of the earliest surviving Persian manual, which is anonymous and known from one manuscript."}],texts:[{title:"Khvābguzārī",note:"Anonymous, Persian, sixth or seventh Islamic century; the earliest substantial Persian dream manual, surviving in a single manuscript. Edited by Īraj Afshār (Tehran, 1346/1967)."},{title:"Kitāb al-Bishāra wa'l-nidhāra, 'Good Tidings and Warning'",note:"Abū Saʿd al-Kharkūshī of Nishapur, d. 406/1015 or 407/1016 — one of the fullest early Arabic manuals of interpretation, by an author who also wrote the Sufi handbook Tahdhīb al-asrār."},{title:"Taʿbīr al-ruʾyā",note:"Ibn Qutayba, d. 276/889 — a genuinely early treatise, valuable precisely because so much of the material bearing famous names is later than it claims."},{title:"Muntakhab al-kalām fī tafsīr al-aḥlām",note:"Circulates under the name of Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn (d. 110/728) and is a later compilation. Ibn Sīrīn's authority as an interpreter is well attested; the books printed as his are not his."},{title:"Taʿṭīr al-anām fī taʿbīr al-manām",note:"ʿAbd al-Ghanī al-Nābulusī, d. 1143/1731 — the great late compendium, arranged alphabetically by image, continuously reprinted and still in ordinary use."},{title:"Kashf al-asrār, 'The Unveiling of Secrets'",note:"Rūzbihān Baqlī of Shiraz, 1128–1209 — a Sufi master's record of his own visions, in Arabic. Translated by Carl W. Ernst (1997); Arabic text edited by Firoozeh Papan-Matin with Michael Fishbein (Brill, 2006)."}]},{slug:"letter-mysticism",title:"Letter Mysticism",native:"علم حروف",translit:"ʿilm-i ḥurūf — 'the science of letters'",summary:"Numbers hidden in the alphabet, a Persian art of dating events in verse, and a metaphysics that read the human face as scripture.",paragraphs:["The twenty-eight letters of the Arabic alphabet carry numerical values in an order older than the alphabetical order now used: abjad, havvaz, ḥuṭṭī, kalaman, saʿfaṣ, qarashat, thakhadh, ḍaẓagh — units for the first nine, tens for the next nine, hundreds for the next nine, and a thousand for the last. The eastern and Maghribi traditions differ in the assignment of the final six, and Persian follows the eastern. The practice of totalling is ḥisāb al-jummal. Two facts about the Persian case matter for what follows. The first is that the same twenty-eight letters serve for the Qur'anic revelation, which gives the values a theological seriousness they would not otherwise possess. The second is that Persian writes four letters more — pe, che, zhe and gāf — making thirty-two, and that a tradition which finds significance in the number of letters will find a different significance in Persian than in Arabic. Both counts will be put to work.","The chronogram, the mādda-yi tārīkh, is where letter-numerology becomes a Persian art form rather than an imported technique. A word or a hemistich is composed whose letters, totalled, give the year of the event it commemorates. It flourishes from the Timurid period onward and is then ubiquitous: over doorways and on tombstones, at the close of chronicles, on the completion of bridges and the deaths of poets, and it survives into the twentieth century. The classical example is the phrase khāk-i muṣallā, 'the dust of the Muṣallā', naming the ground by the Ruknābād at Shiraz where Ḥāfiẓ is buried — خ 600, ا 1, ک 20, م 40, ص 90, ل 30, ی 10, totalling 791, which is one of the two years given for his death, 791/1389. What makes this an art and not an arithmetical trick is the number of constraints solved at once. The phrase must scan in the metre, must fit its couplet grammatically, must say something true and preferably moving about the event, and must total exactly with no letter to spare. The best chronograms satisfy all four and read as though the arithmetic had happened to them.","Faḍlallāh Astarābādī (740–796/1339/40–1394) took the premise further than anyone before or since. A Persian of Astarabad on the Caspian, from a family of judges, he first made his name as an interpreter of dreams, and came to hold that the divine word is not conveyed by letters but consists of them, and that the letters are legible in the human body — above all in the face. His principal book, the Jāvidān-nāma-yi kabīr, is written neither in Arabic nor in literary Persian but in his native Astarabadi dialect, a choice that is itself part of the doctrine: revelation is not the property of a sacred language. The system works from the twenty-eight letters of the Qur'anic alphabet and the thirty-two of Persian, and, in the form his followers expounded, correlates them with the lines of the face — the hairline, the brows, the lashes, called the 'mother lines', which are counted as seven and doubled to fourteen when the beard grows — so that the human face is scripture and Adam's face was the Qur'an before there was a Qur'an. Faḍlallāh was executed in 796/1394 at Alinja in Nakhchivan on the order of Tīmūr's son Mīrānshāh. His followers were dispersed and hunted; ʿAlī al-Aʿlā carried the teaching into Anatolia, where it left a lasting deposit in Bektashi circles, and the poet Nasīmī was put to death at Aleppo, on a date variously reported and usually placed around 820/1417–18. The modern scholarship is Shahzad Bashir's Fazlallah Astarabadi and the Hurufis (2005) and Orkhan Mir-Kasimov's Words of Power (2015).","Maḥmūd Pasīkhānī (d. 831/1427–28), expelled from the Hurufi community, founded the Nuqṭaviyya on a doctrine of the nuqṭa, the point: the point of earth as the material origin of all bodies, souls returning through cycles of matter rather than surviving them, and history revolving through alternating Arab and Persian epochs. It is a cyclical materialism with a nativist edge, and it was read as such. Nuqtavi calculation pointed to 1002/1593 as the turn of the cycle, and Shāh ʿAbbās I moved against the movement in precisely that year; the Safavid court chronicles, Iskandar Beg Munshī's ʿĀlamārā-yi ʿAbbāsī among them, report an episode in which a Nuqtavi was briefly seated on the throne in the shah's stead on astrological advice and then put to death. The episode is known only from the chronicles, which are not disinterested; it is repeated here as they report it, and its implications are examined by Kathryn Babayan in Mystics, Monarchs, and Messiahs (2002). Meanwhile lettrism also flourished as an entirely respectable court science. Ṣāʾin al-Dīn ʿAlī Turka Iṣfahānī (770–835/1369–1432), of a Shafiʿi judicial family of Isfahan, built a systematic lettrist metaphysics in Neopythagorean terms and made ʿilm al-ḥurūf the queen of the sciences for the Timurid elite; Matthew Melvin-Koushki's work has shown how central, rather than marginal, this was. And in Shiʿi tradition the jafr, the lettrist book of foreknowledge ascribed to Imam Jaʿfar al-Ṣādiq, is held in reverence as a possession of the Imams rather than as a technique available to anyone."],terms:[{native:"ابجد",translit:"abjad",gloss:"The old alphabetical order — abjad, havvaz, ḥuṭṭī, kalaman, saʿfaṣ, qarashat, thakhadh, ḍaẓagh — carrying the numerical values 1 to 1000. Persian follows the eastern arrangement of the final six letters."},{native:"حساب جمل",translit:"ḥisāb-i jummal",gloss:"The reckoning by letter-values: totalling the letters of a word or line to obtain a number. The neutral name for the technique, before any doctrine is attached to it."},{native:"ماده تاریخ",translit:"mādda-yi tārīkh",gloss:"A chronogram: a phrase whose letters total the year of the event it commemorates, composed to scan and to mean. A major Persian art form from the Timurid period onwards."},{native:"خاک مصلی",translit:"khāk-i muṣallā",gloss:"'The dust of the Muṣallā' — the chronogram for the death of Ḥāfiẓ, naming the ground at Shiraz where he lies. Its letters total 791, that is 791/1389."},{native:"حرف",translit:"ḥarf",gloss:"A letter of the alphabet; ʿilm-i ḥurūf is the science built on them. In Hurufi usage the letter is not a sign of the word but the substance of it."},{native:"کلمه",translit:"kalima",gloss:"A word, and in this literature the divine Word; the Hurufi count of thirty-two kalimāt corresponds to the thirty-two letters of the Persian alphabet."},{native:"نقطه",translit:"nuqṭa",gloss:"The point — the dot that distinguishes one Arabic letter from another, the geometrical point, and for the Nuqṭaviyya the material origin from which bodies arise and to which they return."},{native:"جفر",translit:"jafr",gloss:"The lettrist science of foreknowledge, and the book of it ascribed to Imam Jaʿfar al-Ṣādiq. In Shiʿi tradition it is a possession of the Imams, revered rather than practised."}],texts:[{title:"Jāvidān-nāma-yi kabīr",note:"Faḍlallāh Astarābādī, before 796/1394 — the principal Hurufi scripture, written in the author's Astarabadi dialect rather than Arabic or literary Persian. Studied and partly translated in Orkhan Mir-Kasimov, Words of Power (2015)."},{title:"ʿArsh-nāma, 'The Book of the Throne'",note:"Faḍlallāh Astarābādī — a shorter verse statement of the doctrine, widely copied among his followers and one of the routes by which the teaching reached Anatolia."},{title:"Dīvān of Nasīmī",note:"ʿImād al-Dīn Nasīmī, put to death at Aleppo around 820/1417–18 — the Hurufi doctrine turned into lyric, in Azeri Turkish and Persian, and the form in which it circulated most widely."},{title:"Kitāb al-Mafāḥiṣ, 'The Book of Inquiries'",note:"Ṣāʾin al-Dīn ʿAlī Turka Iṣfahānī, d. 835/1432 — his lettrist summa, and the fullest statement of ʿilm al-ḥurūf as a universal science. His better-known Tamhīd al-qavāʿid, a commentary on his ancestor's Qavāʿid al-tawḥīd, belongs rather to the Ibn ʿArabian tradition."},{title:"Tārīkh-i ʿĀlamārā-yi ʿAbbāsī",note:"Iskandar Beg Munshī, completed 1038/1629 — the Safavid court chronicle that reports the suppression of the Nuqṭaviyya in 1002/1593 and the episode of the substitute enthronement. Translated by Roger Savory (1978)."},{title:"al-Muqaddima, the chapter on the science of letters",note:"Ibn Khaldūn, 779/1377 — a sceptical but detailed account of ʿilm al-ḥurūf and sīmiyāʾ as they stood before the Hurufi and Timurid developments, and a useful control on the insiders' claims."}]},{slug:"cosmology",title:"Cosmology",native:"بندهشن",translit:"Bundahišn — 'primal creation'",summary:"Two worlds and a bounded time in which they are mixed; the bridge where the soul meets its own conduct; and the imaginal earth the later tradition placed between the spheres and the senses.",paragraphs:["Zoroastrianism is a living religion, with communities in Iran and among the Parsis of India, and the Pahlavi books compiled in the ninth century under Islamic rule are their inheritance and not an archaeological curiosity. Chief among them is the Bundahišn, 'primal creation', extant in a longer Iranian and a shorter Indian recension and drawing on Avestan material now lost; beside it stand the Dēnkard, the Mēnōg ī Xrad, the Ardā Wirāz Nāmag and the polemical Škand Gumānīg Wizār. The cosmology they set out begins with two: Ohrmazd above in light, knowing; Ahriman below in darkness, ignorant of what is above him until he rises and sees it. Ahriman attacks, and Ohrmazd — who alone foreknows — proposes a pact bounding the conflict in time, because in unlimited time an evil that cannot be destroyed could not be defeated either. This is the pivotal move of the whole system: finite time, zamān ī kanāragōmand, is cut out of boundless time so that the assault may be met and finished. Three ages follow — creation, then gumēzišn, the mixture, in which we live and in which every good thing is adulterated, and then separation, ending in the frašgird, the making-wonderful, when the world is restored. Evil is real, it is not God's, and it has an expiry date. That last clause is what distinguishes the scheme from every dualism that resigns itself to permanent war.","At death the soul remains three nights near the body, and at the dawn of the fourth it goes to the Činwad puhl, the Bridge of the Separator, where Mihr, Sraoša and Rašn preside and Rašn holds the scales. There the soul meets its own dēn. The word means conscience, religion, and the sum of what a person has done, and it comes to meet the righteous soul in the form of a maiden more beautiful than any it has seen, who tells it, when it asks who she is, that she is its own — that she was already lovely and its good thoughts and words and deeds made her lovelier still. The bridge then broadens for the righteous and narrows for the wicked. The texts are the Hādōxt Nask and the Mēnōg ī Xrad, and the fullest treatment is the Ardā Wirāz Nāmag, in which a righteous priest is sent in trance to traverse heaven and hell and return with a report — a visionary journey of the first importance and, for the community that preserves it, a devotional text rather than a literary one.","Zurwān, boundless time, is a genuine name in the Zoroastrian pantheon, and there is attached to it a myth that must be handled with care. In it Zurvan sacrifices for a thousand years to obtain a son, doubts for a moment whether the sacrifice will avail, and so begets two: Ahriman out of the doubt, born first and holding a promise of prior rule, and Ohrmazd out of the sacrifice. The myth is attested chiefly not in Zoroastrian books but in external witnesses hostile to the religion — the Armenian Eznik of Kolb in the fifth century, the Syriac Theodore bar Konai later — writers with an interest in showing that the Persians were monists after all, or that their dualism was incoherent. R. C. Zaehner built from these reports, in Zurvan: A Zoroastrian Dilemma (1955), the picture of a distinct Zurvanite sect dominant at the Sasanian court. Later scholarship, Shaul Shaked's and Albert de Jong's in particular, has been considerably more cautious, treating Zurvanism as a mythological and speculative current within Zoroastrianism rather than a church with a membership. Zoroastrian communities today do not hold the myth, and it is not part of the tradition as its adherents receive it; that should be said as plainly as the myth is reported.","Islamic Persia inherited a second cosmology and set it beside the first. Below the moon, the four elements in their spheres; above it, the concentric heavens of the planets and the fixed stars and the outermost sphere; and animating the whole, the Fārābian and Avicennan chain of ten intellects, each with its soul and its sphere, the tenth being the Giver of Forms that governs our world. Persian cosmographies made this common property — Muḥammad b. Maḥmūd b. Aḥmad Ṭūsī's ʿAjāyib al-makhlūqāt, written in the third quarter of the twelfth century for a Seljuq patron and edited by Manūchihr Sutūda, and later the Persian versions of Qazwīnī, illustrated for centuries. Suhrawardī replaced the intellects with graded lights and inserted the ʿālam al-mithāl between the spheres and the senses, which gave the visionary an address. And the Shaykhi school of Shaykh Aḥmad al-Aḥsāʾī (1166–1241/1753–1826) and Sayyid Kāẓim Rashtī (d. 1259/1843) gave that world a geography: hūrqalyā, a term of probable Syriac and Harranian ancestry, the earth of the eighth clime, where the resurrection body subsists and where the Hidden Imam has his cities Jābulqā and Jābulsā. The doctrine distinguishes a body of flesh, which is left behind, from a subtler body that is raised, and locates the latter in hurqalyā rather than in the world of the senses. Henry Corbin, in Terre céleste et corps de résurrection (1960), translated as Spiritual Body and Celestial Earth (1977), drew a single line from the Mazdean xvarənah through the imaginal world to the Shaykhi earth and called it one continuous Iranian imagination. It is a beautiful thesis and it has been criticised, justly, for making 'Iran' into an essence and for reading Islamic material through a Mazdean lens that the material does not itself hold up. Shaykhism has adherents in Iran and Iraq today, and hurqalyā is their doctrine before it is anyone's category."],terms:[{native:"اورمزد",translit:"Ohrmazd",gloss:"Ahura Mazdā, the Wise Lord, in the Middle Persian form as it is written in New Persian script; the creator, dwelling in light, whose foreknowledge sets the bound to the conflict."},{native:"اهریمن",translit:"Ahriman",gloss:"Angra Mainyu, the Destructive Spirit; not a fallen creature but an uncreated adversary, ignorant rather than merely wicked, and destined to be undone rather than reconciled."},{native:"زروان",translit:"Zurvān",gloss:"Time. Zurwān ī akanārag, boundless time, is a genuine divine name; the myth in which he fathers both spirits reaches us mainly through Armenian and Syriac polemicists and is not held by Zoroastrian communities today."},{native:"gumēzišn",translit:"gumēzišn",gloss:"'Mixture' — the present age, in which good and evil are adulterated in every existing thing. Given in scholarly Latin transliteration, not native script: Pahlavi script is ambiguous and thinly supported in Unicode, and a guessed writing would be worse than an honest transliteration."},{native:"Činwad puhl",translit:"Činwad puhl",gloss:"The Bridge of the Separator, crossed at the fourth dawn after death under the judgement of Mihr, Sraoša and Rašn. Given in Latin transliteration for the same reason; the New Persian forms vary from text to text."},{native:"دین",translit:"dēn",gloss:"Conscience, religion, and the sum of a life's acts; the same spelling serves New Persian dīn, 'religion'. At the bridge the soul meets its dēn as a maiden who says that his deeds made her what she is."},{native:"هورقلیا",translit:"hūrqalyā",gloss:"The imaginal earth of the eighth clime in Shaykhi doctrine, where the resurrection body subsists; a term of probable Syriac and Harranian ancestry, adopted by Shaykh Aḥmad al-Aḥsāʾī."},{native:"عناصر اربعه",translit:"ʿanāṣir-i arbaʿa",gloss:"The four elements — fire, air, water, earth — occupying the sublunary spheres beneath the celestial ones; the Greek inheritance that Persian cosmography set alongside the older Iranian scheme."}],texts:[{title:"The Greater (Iranian) Bundahišn",note:"Pahlavi, ninth century, drawing on the lost Avestan Dāmdād Nask: creation, the assault, the bounded time, the celestial order and the eschatology. Edition and translation by Domenico Agostini and Samuel Thrope (2020)."},{title:"Dēnkard",note:"'Acts of the Religion', compiled in the ninth century by Ādurfarnbag ī Farroxzādān and continued by Ādurbād ī Ēmēdān — the largest surviving Pahlavi work and the summary of the tradition's own theology."},{title:"Mēnōg ī Xrad, 'The Spirit of Wisdom'",note:"A Pahlavi catechism in question and answer, containing one of the fullest accounts of the fate of the soul at the Činwad bridge and of the meeting with the dēn."},{title:"Ardā Wirāz Nāmag",note:"The visionary journey of the righteous Wirāz through heaven, the place of the mixed and hell, sent in trance and returned to report. Preserved in Pahlavi and in later Persian verse redactions."},{title:"ʿAjāyib al-makhlūqāt wa gharāyib al-mawjūdāt",note:"Muḥammad b. Maḥmūd b. Aḥmad Ṭūsī, sixth/twelfth century — a Persian cosmography of the spheres, the elements and their inhabitants, and the channel by which the Ptolemaic and Avicennan world-picture reached a general readership. Edited by Manūchihr Sutūda (Tehran, 1345/1966)."},{title:"Sharḥ al-Ziyāra al-jāmiʿa al-kabīra",note:"Shaykh Aḥmad al-Aḥsāʾī, d. 1241/1826 — his most extensive work, and the principal statement of the Shaykhi doctrines of the subtle body and the earth of hurqalyā."}]},{slug:"esoteric-poetry",title:"Esoteric Poetry",native:"غزل",translit:"ghazal",summary:"A closed vocabulary of wine, tavern and beloved; the poem written expressly to unlock it; and the reasons the lock was made not to open cleanly.",paragraphs:["The Persian ghazal is short — five to fifteen couplets — monorhymed, frequently carrying a radīf, a repeated word after the rhyme, and signed with the poet's pen-name in the closing line. Its nouns are a nearly closed set, and the set is what this essay is about. There is wine, may or bāda or sharāb, and the sāqī who pours it; the kharābāt, the 'ruins', the tavern quarter outside the law; the pīr-i mughān, the Magian elder who keeps it, and the mughbacha, the Magian's boy who serves in it. It is worth pausing on those two, because the sociology precedes the symbolism: Zoroastrians could lawfully make and sell wine where Muslims could not, so the tavern-keeper of a Persian city really was likely to be a Zoroastrian, and the poets are describing an actual arrangement before they are describing anything else. It is equally worth saying that the Magian of the ghazal is thereafter a literary type, shaped by what the poet needed him to mean, and is not a description of the Zoroastrian community, then or now. There is the but, the idol, and the butkhāna, the idol-temple; the zunnār, the girdle worn by Christians under Islamic law and adopted by the poet as a badge of cheerful infidelity; the khirqa, the Sufi's patched cloak, which is perpetually being pawned for drink. There is the beloved's zulf, the tress, and khāl, the mole, and khaṭṭ, the first down on the cheek — which is also the ordinary word for handwriting — and abrū, the eyebrow. There is the rind, the libertine who is nearer to God than the pious, and the muḥtasib, the market-inspector who enforces sobriety and is the poems' standing figure of hypocrisy. And there is a grammatical fact that no translation preserves: Persian has no gender, and the pronoun ū serves for he, she and it, so that the beloved's identity is left open by the language itself before the poet has done anything at all.","The key to the vocabulary was written by a poet who was asked for it. Maḥmūd Shabistarī, of Shabistar near Tabriz (c. 687–740/1288–1340), composed the Gulshan-i Rāz, 'The Rose-Garden of Mystery', in Shawwāl 717 / December 1317: about a thousand couplets, in answer to seventeen questions sent to the Sufis of Azerbaijan by Rukn al-Dīn Amīr Ḥusaynī Harawī (d. 718/1318). The later questions ask outright what the poets mean by the eye and the lip, the tress and the mole, wine and the candle and the beautiful figure, the idol, the girdle and Christianity — that is, the questioner had noticed that the lyric tradition was using a technical vocabulary and wanted it construed. Shabistarī construes it: the tress is the multiplicity that veils the face of unity, the mole is the point of the essence at the centre, wine is the intoxication that precedes annihilation, the idol is the manifest form in which the Real is worshipped by those who do not know what they are worshipping. But he does not present this as decipherment. His position is that these images are not a cipher laid over a plain meaning; they are the only available language for states that have no plain meaning to lay bare, and the sensory image is the necessary vehicle rather than a disposable wrapper. Shams al-Dīn Muḥammad Lāhījī, a Nurbakhshi shaykh of Shiraz (d. 912/1506–7), wrote the great commentary, Mafātīḥ al-iʿjāz fī sharḥ-i Gulshan-i Rāz, which is a summa in its own right; E. H. Whinfield translated the poem in 1880, and Leonard Lewisohn's Beyond Faith and Infidelity (1995) is the modern study.","The argument over literal and symbolic reading is old, and the commentators on Ḥāfiẓ divide on it in a particularly instructive way. Two of the three great Ottoman commentaries, those of Sürûrî and Şemʿî, read him mystically throughout. The third, by Sûdî of Bosnia (d. c. 1004/1596), is resolutely grammatical: it glosses idiom, syntax and lexis, identifies the metre, and declines allegory almost entirely — which makes it the most useful of the three to a reader trying to construe a line and the least edifying to a reader trying to be improved by one. In Iran the traditional reading has been mystical, and it is embedded in practice rather than only in criticism: Ḥāfiẓ's tomb at Shiraz is a place of visitation, and the fāl-i Ḥāfiẓ, opening the Dīvān at random for guidance, is an ordinary domestic act performed at Nowruz and at Yalda in houses that would not describe themselves as Sufi at all. Modern scholarship has pulled in several directions at once — Julie Scott Meisami reading the court poetry as rhetorical and panegyric before it is anything else, Michael Hillmann on the question of whether the ghazal has thematic unity, J. T. P. de Bruijn tracing Persian Sufi poetry as a historical formation with datable stages, and the essays collected in Leonard Lewisohn's Hafiz and the Religion of Love in Classical Persian Poetry (2010).","The last thing to say is that Persian rhetoric has a name for what is happening, which suggests the effect was intended. Īhām, 'causing to imagine', is the figure in which a word is chosen for having a near and a far sense, and the context is built to support both without deciding between them. It is taught in the manuals of badīʿ as a prized ornament, not tolerated as an ambiguity; Ḥāfiẓ is its acknowledged master, and a great many of his most famous lines are unresolvable in principle rather than merely obscure in fact. A poem built on īhām is not an encrypted message awaiting the right key. It is an instrument for holding two readings in suspension, and the suspension is the state the poem exists to produce in the reader. There were also plain reasons of prudence: wine was forbidden and the tavern real, the beloved was frequently unnameable, and a poet in fourteenth-century Shiraz was read by the Sufi, the jurist, the courtier and the wine-drinker out of the same manuscript, with every incentive to write lines that would not settle for any of them. To ask whether Ḥāfiẓ 'really' meant God or 'really' meant wine is to ask a question the form was constructed to refuse, and the refusal is not an evasion. It is the poem's subject."],terms:[{native:"غزل",translit:"ghazal",gloss:"The lyric form: five to fifteen monorhymed couplets, often with a radīf, closing with the poet's pen-name. The vehicle of nearly all Persian symbolic poetry."},{native:"می",translit:"may",gloss:"Wine; with bāda and sharāb, the central image of the tradition — the intoxication that dissolves the self-regarding self, and also, in the same line, a forbidden drink in an actual tavern."},{native:"خرابات",translit:"kharābāt",gloss:"'The ruins' — the tavern quarter outside the city and outside the law. In the symbolic reading, the state of ruin in which the self's structures have collapsed and something else can be built."},{native:"پیر مغان",translit:"pīr-i mughān",gloss:"The Magian elder, keeper of the tavern; symbolically the true master, set against the official piety of the mosque. The figure has a historical root, since Zoroastrians could lawfully sell wine, but in the poetry it is a literary type and not a portrait of a community."},{native:"رند",translit:"rind",gloss:"The libertine, the rogue who drinks and is honest about it — Ḥāfiẓ's ideal figure, opposed throughout to the zāhid, the ostentatious ascetic, and to the muḥtasib who polices him."},{native:"زلف",translit:"zulf",gloss:"The beloved's tress or curling lock; in Shabistarī's gloss, the multiplicity of manifested things which both veils the unity of the face and is the only means by which it is seen."},{native:"خال",translit:"khāl",gloss:"The mole on the beloved's cheek; read as the point of the divine essence, the black indivisible centre about which the whole of manifestation is arranged."},{native:"ایهام",translit:"īhām",gloss:"'Causing to imagine' — the rhetorical figure of deliberate double meaning, in which a word's near and far senses are both supported by the context. A prized ornament of Persian badīʿ, and Ḥāfiẓ's signature."}],texts:[{title:"Dīvān of Ḥāfiẓ",note:"Shams al-Dīn Muḥammad Ḥāfiẓ of Shiraz, d. 791 or 792/1389–90; some five hundred ghazals, transmitted in widely varying recensions. Standard editions by Muḥammad Qazvīnī and Qāsim Ghanī (1941) and by Parvīz Nātil Khānlarī (1359/1980)."},{title:"Gulshan-i Rāz, 'The Rose-Garden of Mystery'",note:"Maḥmūd Shabistarī, Shawwāl 717 / December 1317 — c. 1,000 couplets answering seventeen questions from Rukn al-Dīn Amīr Ḥusaynī Harawī, the last of which ask directly what the poets' images mean. Translated by E. H. Whinfield (1880)."},{title:"Mafātīḥ al-iʿjāz fī sharḥ-i Gulshan-i Rāz",note:"Shams al-Dīn Muḥammad Lāhījī, Nurbakhshi shaykh of Shiraz, d. 912/1506–7 — the classic commentary on Shabistarī and one of the fullest expositions of the symbolic lexicon in Persian."},{title:"Sharḥ-i Dīvān-i Ḥāfiẓ",note:"Aḥmad Sûdî of Bosnia, d. c. 1004/1596 — a grammatical and lexical commentary in Ottoman Turkish that declines allegorical reading, and the natural counterweight to the mystical commentaries of Sürûrî and Şemʿî."},{title:"Lamaʿāt, 'Divine Flashes'",note:"Fakhr al-Dīn ʿIrāqī, d. 688/1289 — Persian prose and verse on love in the school of Ibn ʿArabī, and one of the works that made the erotic vocabulary explicitly metaphysical."},{title:"Rashf al-alḥāẓ fī kashf al-alfāẓ",note:"Sharaf al-Dīn Ḥusayn ibn Ulfatī Tabrīzī, eighth/fourteenth century — a Persian glossary of the symbolic vocabulary of the poets, and evidence that readers of the period wanted the terms explained as much as readers now do."}]}]};export{e as default};
